<title>Vertebral Compression Fracture: Management.</title>
<graphic href="eee0826-w0001.gif" />
<cell type="start" id="cell-1">
<label>Acute low back pain with suspected vertebral compression fracture (VCF)</label>
<path target="cell-2"></path>
</cell>
<cell type="decision" id="cell-2">
<label>Neurological deficits?</label>
<path target="cell-3">Yes</path>
<path target="cell-4">No</path>
</cell>
<cell type="decision" id="cell-4">
<label>History of cancer?</label>
<path target="cell-3">Yes</path>
<path target="cell-5">No</path>
</cell>
<cell type="decision" id="cell-5">
<label>One or more risk factors for cancer or osteoporotic fracture (previous VCF, osteoporosis, steroid use, older than 50 years, unexplained weight loss, failure to improve after 1 month)?</label>
<path target="cell-6">Yes</path>
<path target="cell-7">No</path>
</cell>
<cell type="action" id="cell-7">
<label>Treat as musculoskeletal low back pain. Consider imaging if patient does not improve.</label>
</cell>
<cell type="action" id="cell-6">
<label>Plain X-ray.</label>
</cell>
<cell type="action" id="cell-3">
<label>CT or MRI.</label>
</cell>
</algorithm>
